`,e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["The best guess for B moves toward what you measured at A, by a fraction set by the correlation. The spread shrinks from 1 to ",e.jsx(s.span,{className:"katex",children:e.jsx(s.math,{xmlns:"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML",children:e.jsxs(s.semantics,{children:[e.jsx(s.mrow,{children:e.jsx(s.msqrt,{children:e.jsxs(s.mrow,{children:[e.jsx(s.mn,{children:"1"}),e.jsx(s.mo,{children:"−"}),e.jsxs(s.msup,{children:[e.jsx(s.mi,{children:"ρ"}),e.jsx(s.mn,{children:"2"})]})]})})}),e.jsx(s.annotation,{encoding:"application/x-tex",children:"\\sqrt{1-\\rho^2}"})]})})}),", which is small only when ",e.jsx(s.span,{className:"katex",children:e.jsx(s.math,{xmlns:"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML",children:e.jsxs(s.semantics,{children:[e.jsx(s.mrow,{children:e.jsx(s.mi,{children:"ρ"})}),e.jsx(s.annotation,{encoding:"application/x-tex",children:"\\rho"})]})})})," is close to 1. With ",e.jsx(s.span,{className:"katex",children:e.jsx(s.math,{xmlns:"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML",children:e.jsxs(s.semantics,{children:[e.jsxs(s.mrow,{children:[e.jsx(s.mi,{children:"ρ"}),e.jsx(s.mo,{children:"="}),e.jsx(s.mn,{children:"0.8"})]}),e.jsx(s.annotation,{encoding:"application/x-tex",children:"\\rho = 0.8"})]})})})," the spread drops to 0.6; with ",e.jsx(s.span,{className:"katex",children:e.jsx(s.math,{xmlns:"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML",children:e.jsxs(s.semantics,{children:[e.jsxs(s.mrow,{children:[e.jsx(s.mi,{children:"ρ"}),e.jsx(s.mo,{children:"="}),e.jsx(s.mn,{children:"0.3"})]}),e.jsx(s.annotation,{encoding:"application/x-tex",children:"\\rho = 0.3"})]})})})," it barely moves, to 0.95."]}),`
`,e.jsx(Ne,{children:"Each dot is one possible pair of values at A and B. The dashed line is the value measured at A; the orange dot on it is the best guess for B, and the thick bar is the range that holds 95% of the possibilities. On the right, what we believe about B before the measurement (dashed) and after it (solid), drawn to the same scale."}),`
`,e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["Set ",e.jsx(s.span,{className:"katex",children:e.jsx(s.math,{xmlns:"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML",children:e.jsxs(s.semantics,{children:[e.jsx(s.mrow,{children:e.jsx(s.mi,{children:"ρ"})}),e.jsx(s.annotation,{encoding:"application/x-tex",children:"\\rho"})]})})})," to 0, and the measurement at A changes nothing about B. Set it to 0.99, and B is pinned almost exactly to A. This rule, applied to many numbers at once, is all a Gaussian process does."]}),`
`,e.jsx(s.h2,{children:"From two numbers to a whole curve"}),`
`,e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["A curve is a long list of numbers: the value at ",e.jsx(s.span,{className:"katex",children:e.jsx(s.math,{xmlns:"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML",children:e.jsxs(s.semantics,{children:[e.jsxs(s.mrow,{children:[e.jsx(s.mi,{children:"x"}),e.jsx(s.mo,{children:"="}),e.jsx(s.mn,{children:"0"})]}),e.jsx(s.annotation,{encoding:"application/x-tex",children:"x = 0"})]})})}),", at ",e.jsx(s.span,{className:"katex",children:e.jsx(s.math,{xmlns:"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML",children:e.jsxs(s.semantics,{children:[e.jsxs(s.mrow,{children:[e.jsx(s.mi,{children:"x"}),e.jsx(s.mo,{children:"="}),e.jsx(s.mn,{children:"0.1"})]}),e.jsx(s.annotation,{encoding:"application/x-tex",children:"x = 0.1"})]})})}),", at ",e.jsx(s.span,{className:"katex",children:e.jsx(s.math,{xmlns:"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML",children:e.jsxs(s.semantics,{children:[e.jsxs(s.mrow,{children:[e.jsx(s.mi,{children:"x"}),e.jsx(s.mo,{children:"="}),e.jsx(s.mn,{children:"0.2"})]}),e.jsx(s.annotation,{encoding:"application/x-tex",children:"x = 0.2"})]})})}),", and so on. An uncertain curve is a long list of uncertain numbers. To describe it the way we described A and B, we need a bell curve for each value, which is easy (mean 0, spread 1), and a correlation for every pair of values, which is the interesting part."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["For a smooth quantity, nearby inputs should have similar outputs, and inputs far apart can be unrelated. One rule that says exactly this makes the correlation fall with the distance ",e.jsx(s.span,{className:"katex",children:e.jsx(s.math,{xmlns:"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML",children:e.jsxs(s.semantics,{children:[e.jsx(s.mrow,{children:e.jsx(s.mi,{children:"d"})}),e.jsx(s.annotation,{encoding:"application/x-tex",children:"d"})]})})})," between two inputs:"]}),`
`,e.jsx(s.span,{className:"katex",children:e.jsx(s.math,{xmlns:"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML",display:"block",children:e.jsxs(s.semantics,{children:[e.jsxs(s.mrow,{children:[e.jsx(s.mi,{children:"ρ"}),e.jsx(s.mo,{stretchy:"false",children:"("}),e.jsx(s.mi,{children:"d"}),e.jsx(s.mo,{stretchy:"false",children:")"}),e.jsx(s.mo,{children:"="}),e.jsx(s.mi,{children:"exp"}),e.jsx(s.mo,{children:"⁡"}),e.jsx(s.mtext,{children:" ⁣"}),e.jsxs(s.mrow,{children:[e.jsx(s.mo,{fence:"true",children:"("}),e.jsx(s.mo,{children:"−"}),e.jsxs(s.mfrac,{children:[e.jsxs(s.msup,{children:[e.jsx(s.mi,{children:"d"}),e.jsx(s.mn,{children:"2"})]}),e.jsxs(s.mrow,{children:[e.jsx(s.mn,{children:"2"}),e.jsxs(s.msup,{children:[e.jsx(s.mi,{mathvariant:"normal",children:"ℓ"}),e.jsx(s.mn,{children:"2"})]})]})]}),e.jsx(s.mo,{fence:"true",children:")"})]}),e.jsx(s.mi,{mathvariant:"normal",children:"."})]}),e.jsx(s.annotation,{encoding:"application/x-tex",children:"\\rho(d) = \\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{d^2}{2\\ell^2}\\right)."})]})})}),`
`,e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["The number ",e.jsx(s.span,{className:"katex",children:e.jsx(s.math,{xmlns:"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML",children:e.jsxs(s.semantics,{children:[e.jsx(s.mrow,{children:e.jsx(s.mi,{mathvariant:"normal",children:"ℓ"})}),e.jsx(s.annotation,{encoding:"application/x-tex",children:"\\ell"})]})})})," is the ",e.jsx(s.em,{children:"length scale"}),": the distance over which values stay strongly related. At a distance of one length scale the correlation is 0.61. At three length scales it is 0.01, and the two values have practically nothing to say about each other. This rule is called the squared exponential kernel, and Rasmussen and Williams (2006, eq. 2.31) use it as their running example. A ",e.jsx(s.em,{children:"kernel"}),", also called a covariance function, is the rule that gives the correlation (more generally, the covariance) for any pair of inputs."]}),`
`,e.jsx(s.p,{children:"That is enough to draw random curves. Pick 121 inputs between 0 and 10, fill in the correlation of every pair with the rule above, and draw one sample from the resulting 121-dimensional bell curve. Each sample is a whole curve."}),`
`,e.jsx(Le,{children:"Four curves drawn at random from a Gaussian process whose correlation rule is shown on the right; the orange dot marks a distance of one length scale. The shaded band holds 95% of the draws at every input. There is no data yet, so every input is equally uncertain."}),`
`,e.jsx(s.p,{children:"Move the length scale. Short length scales give curves that wiggle, because values only a little apart are already nearly unrelated. Long ones give slow, gentle curves. Nothing else changes: the figure reuses the same random numbers for every setting, and the rule alone decides how they are tied together."}),`
`,e.jsx(s.p,{children:'Those draws come from a Gaussian process. The textbook defines one as "a collection of random variables, any finite number of which have a joint Gaussian distribution" (Rasmussen & Williams, 2006, p. 13). Pick any inputs you like, two or 121 or a million, and the outputs there follow a bell curve in that many dimensions, with correlations given by the kernel. Two things specify a Gaussian process completely: a mean function (here 0 everywhere, our guess before any data) and the kernel.'}),`
